# Runtime State and Debugging

This Livebook uses only public APIs. It shows three different views of one live
Agent:

- The committed Agent value and revision.
- The current Agent Server phase and runtime status.
- A bounded, in-memory history of recent runtime decisions.

The Agent debug buffer is separate from semantic logging. The buffer and
semantic events do not contain full Signals, Agent state copies, or raw errors.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Define and start an Agent

```elixir
defmodule DebugGuide.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "debug_guide_counter"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/guides/debug"

    route "counter.add" do
      action %{amount: amount},
        name: "debug_guide_add",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + amount}}
      end

      define :add, args: [:amount]
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

{:ok, counter} =
  Jido.start_agent(
    DebugGuide.Counter,
    id: "debug-counter",
    debug: true,
    debug_max_events: 20
  )
```

`debug: true` enables the event buffer for this Agent. `debug_max_events` must
be a positive integer. Its default is 500.

## Run a Turn

```elixir
{:ok, committed} = DebugGuide.Counter.add(counter, 2)
committed.state
```

## Read the public views

```elixir
agent = Jido.AgentServer.agent(counter)
snapshot = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(counter)
status = Jido.AgentServer.status(counter)
children = Jido.AgentServer.children(counter)

%{
  agent: agent,
  snapshot: snapshot,
  status: status,
  children: children
}
```

Use each view for one purpose:

| Function | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `agent/1` | The current committed `Jido.Agent` value |
| `snapshot/1` | `%{agent: agent, state_version: revision}` |
| `status/1` | A narrow view of current runtime work |
| `children/1` | Current Agent and Plugin child ownership |
| `plugin_state/2` | One declared Plugin-owned field from the Agent state map |

The snapshot is a live runtime value. It is not a Codec document or a durable
checkpoint.

The status contains these top-level fields:

- `phase`: `:initializing`, `:idle`, `:admitting`, `:running`, or `:directing`.
- `agent_id` and `state_version`.
- `admission`: postponed work, its limit, and mailbox size.
- `runtime`: partition, parent, child, error, and idle-lifecycle information.
- `active`: the current Turn identity and revision information, or `nil`.

Do not read private `:gen_statem` data. Its shape is not a public contract.

## Read recent events

```elixir
{:ok, events} = Jido.AgentServer.recent_events(counter, limit: 10)

Enum.map(events, fn entry ->
  outcome = entry.metadata[:outcome] || %{}

  %{
    event: entry.event,
    at: entry.at,
    status: outcome[:status],
    stage: outcome[:stage],
    committed?: outcome[:committed?]
  }
end)
```

Events are newest-first. The buffer can contain:

- `:turn_committed` after a new revision becomes live.
- `:turn_completed` after a successful Turn settles.
- `:turn_failed` after a failed, cancelled, timed-out, or indeterminate Turn.
- `:directive_failed` after one post-commit Directive fails.
- `:error_signal_delivery_failed` when error-policy Signal delivery fails.
- `:definition_upgrade_completed` after a validated definition replacement becomes live.
- `:definition_upgrade_failed` when a definition replacement does not change live state.

A terminal Turn entry contains a bounded outcome map. It includes Turn, Agent,
and Signal IDs, status, stage, commit state, revisions, Directive counts,
timing, and a safe error projection when applicable. It does not contain the
complete `Jido.Agent.Turn.Outcome`, full Signals, or Agent state.

The debug outcome uses the runtime Outcome vocabulary. Its statuses are
`:succeeded`, `:failed`, `:cancelled`, `:timed_out`, and `:indeterminate`. Its
stages can include `:prepare`, `:execute`, `:finalize`, `:commit`, and
`:directive`. Semantic Telemetry maps these values to its smaller public status
and stage vocabulary.

The event buffer is for a short investigation. It is not durable, and it is not
an audit record. A restart starts a new buffer.

## Enable and disable the Agent buffer

You can enable the buffer after the Agent starts:

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.set_debug(counter, false)
{:error, :debug_not_enabled} = Jido.AgentServer.recent_events(counter)

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.set_debug(counter, true)
{:ok, _committed} = DebugGuide.Counter.add(counter, 3)
{:ok, new_events} = Jido.AgentServer.recent_events(counter)
```

Disabling the buffer clears it. Enabling it does not add events for work that
already finished.

For a stable Agent Ref, use the generated instance functions
`MyApp.Jido.set_agent_debug/3` and `MyApp.Jido.recent_events/3`. These functions
resolve the current local Agent activation before they call the same buffer
API.

## Change semantic log detail

Instance debug mode controls the semantic logger. It does not control the Agent
event buffer.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.debug(:on)
:on = Jido.debug()

:ok = Jido.debug(:verbose)
:ok = Jido.debug(:off)
```

`:on` selects `:interesting` semantic logs for the instance. `:verbose` selects
`:all`. `:off` removes the instance override and uses the global semantic log
configuration. All modes keep the same metadata filtering.

## Stop the example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(counter)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

Continue with [Observe Agent Turns](observe-agent-turns.html), and then read
[Telemetry, Tracing, and Logs](telemetry-tracing-and-logs.html).
